Q&A on Double Ninth Festival|How to view US January Non-farm Payroll data unexpectedly high and future trend? - Economy in Review - Professional Financial News Website
Q: How do you see the January non-agricultural employment data in the United States exceeding expectations and future trends?
A: On February 3, the U.S. Department of Labor released the non-farm payroll data for January 2023, showing an increase of 5.17 million in non-farm jobs, significantly higher than the market expectation of 1.89 million; the unemployment rate fell to 3.4%, its lowest level in nearly 50 years; and hourly wages grew at a year-over-year rate of 4.4% and at a month-over-month rate of 0.3%, slightly slowing.
Overall, there were many one-off and technical factors influencing this data, such as re-engagement of faculty and staff returning from strike, revisions to survey and statistical scopes, updates to seasonally adjusted factors. Going forward, these adjustments may be made (referencing the January 2022 revision of non-farm employment, which was reduced from 504,000 to 364,000), implying that non-farm employment may not be as robust as the data suggests.
Structurally, the impact of layoffs in high-tech enterprises is still relatively low, and there has been a significant recovery in new employment in high-contact services such as leisure hotels, education and health, and data show that labour gaps remain in the services sector.
In combination with the recovery of equally over-expected services PMI, job openings and labour gap data, and the strength of consumer credit data, we judge that the services sector in the United States has been rapidly repaired after extreme weather and holidays.
We believe that the US labor market’s demand-supply tension will not improve without going through a recession.
Currently, the US labor market still has a shortfall of about 5 million in supply, and participation rates are rising slowly, especially with labor force participation for those aged 25-54 already near its pre-pandemic highs.
Furthermore, strong consumer demand, driven by robust excess savings and consumer credit among American residents, will further delay the impact of tightening policies on the job market.
Persistent tightness in the US labor market supply and demand will be the backdrop to the US economy in 2023.
The overlap is now nearing the end of the rate hike, inflation expectations are repeating, and we are not optimistic about the continued decline in wage growth.
Before this data release, expectations for the Federal Reserve’s path of interest rate hikes in the US stock, bond, and currency markets were too optimistic.
As the inflation data continues to improve, the Fed maintains a fragile balance of market inflation expectations while shortening the rate of interest rates, but the market ignores the Fed's bias towards inflation, not only anticipating an early halt to interest rates, but also anticipating two interest rate cuts by the end of the year.
With labor market supply and demand tight and the impact of austerity policies unknown, we believe the Fed's end-of-year guidance on interest rate hikes is relatively reasonable, with extreme market expectations that adjustments and trades will be crowded or will cause some damage to the market.
Nevertheless, the persistence of wage growth is unlikely to change the current trend of rapid decline in inflation, with the gradual improvement of the M2 data and the leading indicators of the various CPI segments, the US CPI data has moved to a phase of rapid decline since October, and the persistence of wage growth will only form the basis of subsequent inflation data, and current market expectations will be difficult to falsify, either after the next inflation data is published in the market or entering a new round of gambling.
Book for Quickly Understanding Internet Finance
This book is quite superficial and could be seen as an introductory course that helps people quickly get acquainted with the landscape of internet finance; however, to delve deeper, one still needs to follow the clues and explore on their own.
And given that the book was published earlier, the content about cultivating user habits through subsidies in later battles between Alipay and WeChat Pay has not been mentioned, it seems that the share of third-party payments today may not be as small as what was stated in the book.
With people traveling these days, they bring their phones to eat out instead of carrying cash; moreover, banks refuse to provide the high transaction volume that the AC/CRS system requires for payment, which has led some merchants to abandon the UnionPay card.
But I still want to thank the author for writing this introductory book, as it is clear that the author has at least several years of experience in financial observation and is well-versed in the history of both domestic and international finance, which allows them to produce such easily understandable content.
Writing grandiose things is easy, but writing in an understandable way is difficult.
